>
  <p>
    Eczema — atopic dermatitis in its commonest form — is itchy, dry, inflamed skin arising from
    two linked problems: a skin barrier that loses water and lets irritants in, and an immune
    system that over-reacts once they do.
  </p>

  <p>
    It runs in families alongside asthma and allergic rhinitis. In infants it favours the cheeks
    and the outer limbs; in older children and adults it settles in the flexures — the inner
    elbows, behind the knees, the neck, the wrists and the eyelids.
  </p>

  <h2>What makes it flare</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Soap, detergent, bubble bath and anything that strips the skin</li>
    <li>Hot water and long baths</li>
    <li>Wool and synthetic fabric against the skin</li>
    <li>Dry winter air and sudden temperature change</li>
    <li>Sweat and heat in summer</li>
    <li>Dust, pollen and pet dander in some patients</li>
    <li>Stress and poor sleep — the itch-scratch cycle feeds itself</li>
    <li>Infection, particularly staphylococcus, which turns an ordinary flare into a weeping one</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it is assessed</h2>

  <p>
    Clinically, in most cases. The distribution and history are characteristic, and a personal or
    family history of asthma or allergic rhinitis supports it. A KOH mount is worth doing where
    fungus is possible, particularly in the folds. Patch testing is arranged where a contact
    trigger is suspected — a specific pattern, an occupational exposure, or eczema that never
    settles despite good treatment.
  </p>

  <p>
    Swabs are taken when skin looks infected: weeping, crusting, sudden worsening.
  </p>

  <h2>Treatment</h2>

  <h3>Emollients — the part that is routinely underdone</h3>
  <p>
    Applied generously, at least twice a day, and within minutes of bathing. A thicker ointment
    holds moisture better than a light lotion. This is not an optional supporting measure; it is
    the base on which everything else works, and it reduces how much steroid is needed.
  </p>

  <h3>Topical steroids</h3>
  <p>
    Used at the right potency for the site, in courses to bring a flare under control, then
    stepped down. Mild preparations for the face and folds, stronger ones for thicker skin.
    Treating a flare properly and briefly is better than treating it half-heartedly for months.
  </p>

  <h3>Steroid-sparing options</h3>
  <p>
    Topical calcineurin inhibitors — tacrolimus, pimecrolimus — are useful on the face, eyelids
    and folds and for longer-term maintenance where steroids are not suitable.
  </p>

  <h3>When it is more severe</h3>
  <p>
    Phototherapy, or systemic treatment including ciclosporin, methotrexate or newer targeted
    biologic agents, with appropriate monitoring. Antibiotics where there is genuine infection,
    not routinely.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Managing the itch</h3>
    <p>
      Scratching damages the barrier further and drives the next flare, so breaking the cycle
      matters as much as treating the rash. Keep nails short, use cool compresses, treat flares
      early rather than waiting, and avoid the folk remedies that rely on heat or friction — they
      feel satisfying and they make it worse.
    </p>
  </div>
